WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.600
Every winter, survey teams tow dredges along the bottom of the Chesapeake Bay.

2
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.820
They catch blue crabs buried in the mud.

3
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.460
Scientists use the catches to estimate how many crabs live in the whole Bay.

4
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.700
From one winter to the next, the estimate more than doubled.

5
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.760
The Bay did not get bigger.

6
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.500
So why are there lots of crabs some years, and far fewer in others?

7
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.800
A population is all the members of one species living in the same place.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.540
The blue crabs in the Bay are one population.

9
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.720
Births add crabs. So do crabs moving in.

10
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.280
Deaths take crabs away.

11
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.180
So do crabs moving out.

12
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.600
When more are added than lost, the population grows.

13
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.240
When more are lost, it shrinks.

14
00:00:55.083 --> 00:01:00.263
A limiting factor is anything that keeps a population from growing larger.

15
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:04.363
A biotic factor is a living part of the environment.

16
00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:07.623
Big crabs eat many young crabs.

17
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:11.343
An abiotic factor is a nonliving part.

18
00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:16.023
In a very cold winter, more crabs die before spring.

19
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:21.817
Young crabs can hide from big crabs in shallow water and grass.

20
00:01:22.637 --> 00:01:26.537
With less grass, more young crabs need the same hiding places.

21
00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:29.057
That need is called competition.

22
00:01:29.997 --> 00:01:33.737
When a limiting factor changes, the population can change too.

23
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.470
Some causes are natural, like cold winters and floods.

24
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.910
People cause changes too.

25
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:47.750
One is pollution. Another is hard shorelines, which can shrink the shallow water

26
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.070
where young crabs hide.

27
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.030
A third is overharvesting.

28
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.170
That means people catch a species faster than it can replace itself.

29
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.940
In 2008, Virginia and Maryland made a plan.

30
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.520
They would catch about 34% fewer female crabs than the earlier average.

31
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.140
Females carry the eggs, so leaving more of them can mean more young crabs later.

32
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.580
Taking only as many as a population can replace is sustainable harvest.

33
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.080
But a limit is not a promise, and rebuilding can take years.

34
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.080
So why does the Bay have lots of crabs some years and few in others?

35
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.300
The things that limit crabs keep changing.
